    "have": {
      "word": "HAVE",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "To hold in possession or control; to own; as, he has a farm.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To possess, as something which appertains to, is connected with, or affects, one. The earth hath bubbles, as the water has. Shak. He had a fever late. Keats.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To accept possession of; to take or accept. Break thy mind to me in broken English; wilt thou have me Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To get possession of; to obtain; to get. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To cause or procure to be; to effect; to exact; to desire; to require. It had the church accurately described to me. Sir W. Scott. Wouldst thou have me turn traitor also Ld. Lytton.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To bear, as young; as, she has just had a child.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To hold, regard, or esteem. Of them shall I be had in honor. 2 Sam. vi. 22.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To cause or force to go; to take. \"The stars have us to bed.\" Herbert. \"Have out all men from me.\" 2 Sam. xiii. 9.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To take or hold (one's self); to proceed promptly; -- used reflexively, often with ellipsis of the pronoun; as, to have after one; to have at one or at a thing, i. e., to aim at one or at a thing; to attack; to have with a companion. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To be under necessity or obligation; to be compelled; followed by an infinitive. Science has, and will long have, to be a divider and a separatist. M. Arnold. The laws of philology have to be established by external comparison and induction. Earle.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To understand. You have me, have you not Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To put in an awkward position; to have the advantage of; as, that is where he had him. [Slang] form preterit tenses; as, I have loved; I shall have eaten. Originally it was used only with the participle of transitive verbs, and denoted the possession of the object in the state indicated by the participle; as, I have conquered him, I have or hold him in a conquered state; but it has long since lost this independent significance, and is used with the participles both of transitive and intransitive verbs as a device for expressing past time. Had is used, especially in poetry, for would have or should have. Myself for such a face had boldly died. Tennyson. To have a care, to take care; to be on one's guard. conclusion.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
